The hotfix was a tiny lock guard added to the batching path in `serving/runtime/batcher.cc`, serializing per-request parameter writes:

    std::scoped_lock lock(params_write_mutex);
    // preserve param append order per-request

Context (gene f6c9f01c40ad3130, source slack/support/1709123456-fn-params-interleaving-after-canary.json): AcmeCorp escalated after the 10:00 UTC canary (deploy id deploy-20240227-7, variant redwood-v1-chat@v1-quant-p3, rollout=10% of traffic). Symptom: tool calls came back with arguments in swapped order ({session_id, user_id} instead of {user_id, session_id}) or the CALL was omitted entirely; internal repro was 3/10 runs with swapped param order and 2/10 with the tool call dropped. Olga's hypothesis was a race in the continuous-batching path for short sequences where concurrent requests sharing a batching slot merged their param buffers (the 10:00 push was the request-mapper + batching kernel change at ts 1709118000). She pushed the scoped_lock guard "to prevent buffer interleave" alongside Dave's rollback of the request-mapper change on the canary group; the guard merged to the hotfix branch and deployed to the canary edge at 1709123860. 1000 synthetic calls showed no interleaving after the guard on the p3 quant nodes, and Acme confirmed no more missed tool calls after rollback + hotfix. Kevin then drafted a postmortem and added CI regression tests for param ordering plus end-to-end toolcall checks (tail chunk 426811ddd3a5efcf).
